## Reading the results

**Recursive summarization is the only strategy that retains both facts at every
length tested.** That's not a coincidence of the benchmark — it's the only
strategy that looks at *content* rather than *position*. The other three prune
by "where is this in the conversation" or "what role is it," so a fact that
happens to fall outside the kept window/zone is gone, no matter how important
it was.

**Sliding window** and **zone-based pruning** are cheap and predictable, but
they're purely positional: past a certain conversation length, an early fact
has zero chance of survival regardless of importance (0/2 at 100+ messages,
consistently). Zone-based pruning is especially aggressive here (~11 messages
kept regardless of length) because our probe facts land as `tool`/`user`
messages that fall outside the last-N-per-role window once the conversation
moves on.

**Observation masking** keeps every non-tool message and only trims old tool
output, so it does well when the fact is in a user/assistant turn but is not
guaranteed to protect a fact buried in a tool message once enough newer tool
calls arrive.

**Recursive summarization** costs the most latency (it's O(n) sentence
scoring plus a summarizer call) and is the only one that shrinks in true
proportion to conversation length while still holding onto salient content —
because it's the only strategy with any notion of *why* something might
matter. In this benchmark that "why" is a crude keyword heuristic
(`extractive_summarizer` in `benchmark.py`); in production `summarizer_func`
should be a real LLM call, which is dependency-injected into the strategy for
exactly this reason (see `ShortTermMemory.strategy_recursive_summarization`).

## Comparison table (qualitative)

| Strategy | Compression | Fact-safety | Cost | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Sliding window | High, fixed | Low — purely positional | Cheapest | Short-lived tasks where only the last few turns matter |
| Observation masking | Low–medium | Medium — protects non-tool turns | Cheap | Tool-heavy sessions where the reasoning trail matters more than raw tool output |
| Recursive summarization | Scales with length | High — content-aware | Highest (LLM call) | Long sessions where early facts can still be safety-relevant |
| Zone-based pruning | High, fixed | Low — same failure mode as sliding window, per role | Cheap | Predictable per-role retention needs (e.g., always keep all system prompts) |

## Caveat

`key_fact_survival` scores by **word overlap** (≥60% of a fact's meaningful
words present in the kept content), not exact substring matching — so a
paraphrase like "Batch 1042 failed QA because of contamination" still counts
as retaining "Batch 1042 failed QA due to contamination detected in Line 3."
An earlier version of this check used strict substring matching, which would
have wrongly scored a correct paraphrase as "lost."

Recursive summarization's fact retention here is a product of the toy
`extractive_summarizer` in this file being tuned to the same keywords as the
planted facts (`fail`, `recall`, `contaminat`, ...). **The result to trust is
the strategy, not the heuristic**: recursive summarization is the only
strategy in this set with any mechanism for judging *why* something might
matter (it looks at content, not position/role), which is what lets it
protect facts the other three strategies drop by construction. The specific
keyword list is not production-ready — swap in a real LLM call for
`summarizer_func` before relying on this; see `agent/rag_adapter.py` for the
dependency-injection pattern used to wire in an external model elsewhere in
this codebase.
